    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d719">

                <pb facs="0644" n="635" xml:id="pg635"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 M 956/294: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Matthews</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vichy</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-12-19T18:00:00+02:00">December 19,
                            1940—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 20—5:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1158. Both the French press and the American correspondents here have been told
                    to “play down” Murphy’s trip to Africa and to make no reference to any “meeting
                    with Weygand”. Fears of German reaction if much publicity is given his movements
                    and mission were likewise expressed to Murphy by the Minister of Colonies and
                    his assistants, who were most cooperative on the evening before his
                    departure.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Matthews</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
